NAME
  apt - package manager for Freax

SYNOPSIS
  apt update
  apt install PACKAGE...
  apt remove PACKAGE...
  apt upgrade
  apt search PATTERN
  apt show PACKAGE
  apt list [--installed|--upgradable] [PATTERN]
  apt policy PACKAGE
  apt download PACKAGE...
  apt clean
  apt autoremove
  apt sysupdate
  apt sysupgrade

DESCRIPTION
  apt manages Freax packages (.fpkg archives) fetched from one or more
  repositories. It downloads and verifies repository indexes, resolves
  dependencies, and drives dpkg to unpack, configure, and remove
  packages.

  apt requires an internet card (see `man wget`). Package archives and
  indexes are streamed to disk in small chunks; a failed download never
  leaves a partial file in place (temp file plus rename). SHA256 sums
  from the repository index are checked before anything is installed.

  Two separate update mechanisms share this command. Package operations
  (`update`, `install`, `upgrade`, ...) use repositories listed in
  /etc/apt/sources.list. Legacy OS self-update still exists as `apt
  sysupdate` and `apt sysupgrade`, which pull core OS files from a single
  URL.

COMMANDS
  update
    Refresh the package lists from every configured repository. Fetches
    each suite's Release file and component Packages indexes into
    /var/lib/apt/lists. Run this before installing or upgrading so apt
    knows what is available.
  upgrade
    Upgrade every installed package that has a newer candidate. Never
    removes a package or installs a new one to satisfy dependencies;
    packages that would require that are left as-is.
  full-upgrade, dist-upgrade
    Upgrade installed packages, allowing removals and new installs when
    required to complete the upgrade.
  install PACKAGE...
    Install or upgrade the named packages. Dependencies are resolved
    first: the plan is printed, and confirmation is requested unless
    --yes is given. Alternatives and Recommendations are resolved by the
    same rules as Debian.
  reinstall PACKAGE...
    Download and install the newest candidate even if that version is
    already installed.
  remove PACKAGE..., rm
    Remove the named packages. Files listed by the package are deleted,
    but conffiles are kept and the package is left in the config-files
    state. Dependencies that become unused are not removed unless
    autoremove is used.
  purge PACKAGE...
    Remove packages and delete their conffiles too.
  autoremove
    Remove packages that were installed automatically as dependencies
    and are no longer required by anything.
  search PATTERN
    Search package names and descriptions for PATTERN (case-insensitive
    substring). Prints one line per matching package with its name,
    installed state, and short description.
  show PACKAGE, info PACKAGE
    Print the full control fields for a package. If several versions are
    available, all of them are shown.
  list [--installed|--upgradable] [PATTERN]
    List packages. With no option, lists every package known to the
    index. --installed lists packages in the dpkg database, and
    --upgradable lists installed packages with a newer candidate. A
    PATTERN filters by name.
  policy PACKAGE
    Show which candidate version apt would install, which versions are
    available, and which repository they come from.
  download PACKAGE...
    Download the candidate archives into /var/cache/apt/archives without
    installing them. Use --download-only with install to do the same as
    part of an install run.
  clean, autoclean
    Delete downloaded .fpkg archives. clean removes everything;
    autoclean removes only archives no longer needed.
  version
    Print the apt version (works offline).
  sources, source
    Print the effective repository list (works offline). With --os, print
    the legacy OS self-update source instead.
  sysupdate
    Legacy OS self-update: fetch the remote VERSION and manifest,
    compare them with the local /VERSION, and cache the manifest for
    sysupgrade. OS files are not package-managed; this is unchanged from
    earlier releases.
  sysupgrade
    Legacy OS self-update: download every manifest entry and install it
    over the running hard drive. /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow, /etc/hostname,
    /home, and a custom /etc/apt/sources.list are preserved. Kernel and
    boot files take effect on the next in-game reboot.
  help
    Print usage.

OPTIONS
  -y, --yes
    Assume yes to every confirmation prompt. Required for unattended
    installs.
  -f, --force
    Override safety checks. Forces operations that apt would otherwise
    refuse (for example, breaking a package that other installed
    packages depend on) and, for sysupgrade, re-downloads files whose
    local and remote versions already match.
  -q, --quiet
    Print less output.
  -V
    Show package version numbers alongside package names.
  --download-only
    Download the archives but do not unpack or configure them.
  --no-install-recommends
    Do not install Recommended packages; only Depends and Pre-Depends
    are followed.
  --reinstall
    Reinstall packages even when the installed version is already the
    newest.
  --purge
    With remove, delete conffiles as well (same as purge).
  --autoremove
    After an operation, remove automatically installed dependencies that
    are no longer needed.
  --source=URL
    Use URL as the legacy OS update source for this sysupdate/sysupgrade
    run instead of /etc/apt/sources.list.

MAINTAINER SCRIPTS
  A package may ship four Lua scripts, stored under
  /var/lib/dpkg/info/<package>.<name> and run by dpkg at the matching
  point in the package's life cycle:
    preinst   before the package is unpacked
    postinst  after the package is unpacked (usually configuration)
    prerm     before the package is removed
    postrm    after the package is removed
  Each script sees a global `arg` table where arg[1] is the action
  ("install", "configure", "upgrade", "remove", "purge", or
  "abort-upgrade") and arg[2] is the previously installed version during
  an upgrade. A script that fails aborts the operation and leaves the
  package in a half-configured or half-installed state.

CONFFILES
  Files declared as conffiles are configuration files. On upgrade, a
  conffile the user has edited is preserved: the new version is written
  next to it as <path>.dpkg-new for the user to merge. remove keeps
  conffiles and marks the package config-files; purge deletes them.

REPOSITORY LAYOUT
  A repository is a tree rooted at the URL in a `deb` line:
    <uri>/dists/<suite>/Release
    <uri>/dists/<suite>/<component>/binary-all/Packages
    <uri>/pool/<component>/<package>_<version>_all.fpkg
  Freax is single-architecture; every index is binary-all. Release lists
  SHA256 sums for the component indexes, and each Packages stanza lists
  the Filename, Size, and SHA256 of its archive. Packages under pool/ are
  referenced by Filename relative to the repository root.

  To host your own repository, lay files out as above and generate the
  indexes with the tools in this system (see `man dpkg-deb` and
  `man apt-ftparchive`):
    dpkg-deb -b mypkg mypkg_1.0_all.fpkg
    mkdir -p pool/main
    mv mypkg_1.0_all.fpkg pool/main/
    apt-ftparchive packages pool > Packages
    mkdir -p dists/freax/main/binary-all
    mv Packages dists/freax/main/binary-all/Packages
    apt-ftparchive release dists/freax > dists/freax/Release
  Serve the repository root over HTTP, then add a line to
  /etc/apt/sources.list:
    deb http://host/repo freax main

FILES
  /etc/apt/sources.list
  /etc/apt/sources.list.d/*.list
  /var/lib/apt/lists/
  /var/cache/apt/archives/
  /var/lib/dpkg/status
  /var/lib/dpkg/info/
  /VERSION, /manifest

EXAMPLES
  apt update
    Refresh the package lists.

  apt install hello
    Install hello and its dependencies.

  apt upgrade
    Upgrade all installed packages to their newest versions.

  apt list --upgradable
    Show which installed packages have newer versions available.

  apt show hello
    Print hello's control fields.

  apt remove --purge hello
    Remove hello and delete its conffiles.

  apt sysupdate
    Check for a new OS release (legacy updater).

  apt sysupgrade --source=http://192.168.1.5:8000/
    Update the OS from a local test mirror.